the more law enforcement officers that  are willing to acknowledge prohibition  is wrong the better off society is going  to be after Washington state voters  legalized recreational marijuana a brand  new million dollar industry opened up  and while there are a lot of changes  that are still being worked out in the  state one of the biggest may be Drug  Enforcement agents going to work in the  private sector we sat down with Patrick  mone a former special agent for the DEA  he's been in law enforcement for over 15  years but left to work for a company  called privateer holdings a private  equity firm that invests in cannabis why  did you approach privateer holdings in  the first place what were you looking to  do here why does big move well I was  considering a number of job  opportunities I had pre fulfilling  career at the EA but I was ready for  some new challenges and I wasn't looking  in this industry in particular but it  was one particular industry that that in  that interested me what do you see the  future for privateer holdings what are  you going to invest in we're focused on  branding and creating mainstream brands  when it comes down to it it's a  mainstream product that's been  marginalized our goal ultimately is to  provide them with safe clean access to a  tested safe product in so doing will  remove the black market and bring the  entire industry into the light did any  of your co-workers that you worked with  at the DDA what did they have to say  when you you know packed up your your  desk with all of your stuff in a brown  box and we're walking out the door and  you said goodbye what did they say I got  a lot of congratulations I had a lot of  guys express the belief that I was a  trendsetter or a pioneer which is a  little surprising in time I got a lot of  support I got a few guys asking me for  jobs yeah it was it was pretty positive  since joining privateer holdings your  presence is sort of sparked with maybe  called a media frenzy more journalists  are fascinated with this with this move  of yours that you've made why do you  think that people are so interested I  think that I mean we've all heard the  term  the blue line or the blue wall right  that a lot of law enforcement are either  reluctant or prohibited to speak to the  press and so it's a lot of the  fascination I think stems from the  ability to maybe get a bit of an inside  look some of these organizations that  previously were pretty unknown to most  of the public we all know that the end  of prohibition is coming and I think  everyone recognizes that and taking that  first step is often the most difficult  one it just so happened I was the one to  take it have you consumed marihuana  yourself I have but it's been 20 years  in that 20 years did you have  experiences with people you've met or  maybe family members that began to  change your mind maybe that maybe when  you were a young person I don't think it  was experiences with family or friends  really so much as my experiences on the  job and as my as things evolved as my  viewpoint evolved as I spent more time  on the job I began to realize that  enforcing the marijuana statutes as  they're written was was just an  ineffective use of our resources and  that you can say headache that's fine  yeah it wasn't someone it wasn't a  headache because it wasn't you know it's  it's no more work than any other case  that we do right it's just a matter of  did we focus on this issue or do we  focus on another issue and my personal  belief and I think is a belief that was  shared by many of my colleagues was that  we should focus on other issues mostly  the so-called hard drugs the ones that  we feel have an actual law that I feel  have an actual detrimental effect on  society the US has been fighting the  drug war for over 40 years now do you  think the war on drugs will come to an  end at some point I don't know that's  that's a really complex question  obviously I think that the war on  marijuana will come to an end  fairly soon  I think the inevitability has you know  has reached everyone at this point why  not harder drugs then well I mean my own  personal point of view is that drugs  like methamphetamine and heroin have  legitimate observable harmful effects to  the user and people around the user and  you definitely cannot say the same about  cannabis I mean they're they're not in  the same category you can't even talk  about them in the same context so I  think they're totally separate  discussions you know the US Justice  Department's 2013 national drug threat  assessment said that the availability  was increasing for heroin it was also  increasing for methamphetamine I mean  and I know these numbers fluctuates from  year to year to year but what we really  be able to live in a world where we can  stop people from doing this drug no  never I mean our mission the mission at  DEA was really to keep it under control  right I don't think anyone was under the  illusion that we were going to stop it  that we're going to win the war on drugs  or they were trying to minimize the  Associated harms and when it comes down  to it we're sticking our thumbs in the  dike and there's only so much we can do  you know we tried to attack choke points  try to limit supply there's little we  can do about the man as long as the  demand continues to exist there will  always be supply but there will also be  DEA agents like your former self banging  their head against the wall that's true  I'm you know it's a I guess it comes  down to a cost-benefit analysis right or  is the job at the DEA doing producing a  measurable benefit there are some cases  of mine in particular that I'm very  proud of that I can look back at and say  I had a measurable effect on this  community for some period of time before  it bounced back so you know part of DA's  role I think is to try and keep things  under control to a degree  long-term are we gonna succeed who knows  do you ever think you will go back and  work at the DEA no really why not  now that ship has sailed I think I had a  really fulfilling career there I just  felt like it was time for new  opportunities there's really nothing  there for me anymore  I don't regret having work there do you  think that people like you that go and  work in the private sector from coming  from government coming from the DEA do  you fear that it could create a world  where there might be a revolving door  where the regulator's go and work for  the regulated well I mean that's a  pretty common occurrence already right  in government yeah but not necessarily a  great thing right there's some debate  over whether it's a benefit or not I  mean I think everyone agrees in this  particular instance it is a benefit you  know what the future holds I don't know  but I think that the important fact is  going to be you're the important  consideration will be what are the  attitudes and beliefs of these people  the more law enforcement officers that  are willing to acknowledge that  prohibition is wrong the better off  society is going to be what do you say  to the people that say it's not the DEA  agents themselves it's the system it's  the over forty years of the war on drugs  the the mentality that we're going to  crack down on something that we can't  stop ever I say they're not wrong you  know we've we've perpetuated an idea  that that certain behavior is wrong and  I'm concerned that maybe we're not  looking at things objectively anymore  that we need to really stop and look at  are we doing these things for the right  reason and when it comes to marijuana  the policies that are in place right now  official policies I mean guidance to  Washington State excluded are wrong  they're misguided and  hopefully that's going to change soon is  there anything people would be surprised  about working as a special agent for the  DEA hmm okay and I like the vending  machines suck or something I mean you  know I think that there are there's a  certain subset of the population that  views DEA agents as jackbooted thugs  that have an agenda to oppress them or  to who knows right but it's just another  job and there are guys there that are  very competent there are guys there that  are less so but they're all trying to do  the job the best that they can  when it comes to marijuana I think that  they would be might be surprised to know  that the overwhelming majority of the  agents at least that I ever interacted  with don't feel that marijuana is a  priority and you guys discussed this  smoking a bowl after after a long day  right we discussed it I think that's  where the analogy is with that yeah
 

